\section{What the two steps do}{
\strong{Matching.} Donor weights \code{w} sit on the simplex and minimise
\eqn{(X_1 - X_0 w)' V (X_1 - X_0 w)}, where the columns of \code{X} are the
predictors and \code{V} is diagonal. Predictors are standardised by their
cross-unit standard deviation first, so \code{V = I} means "weight every
predictor equally after standardisation" -- the special case of ADH that
does not require the nested optimisation. \code{v_method = "mspe"} instead
searches over diagonal \code{V} for the one minimising pre-period MSPE of the
outcome, which is what \code{Synth} does by default.

\strong{Neither search dominates, which is why \code{"synth"} runs both.} \code{Synth}
optimises \code{V} with \code{optimx} from a single equal-weight start and solves
for \code{w} with an interior-point QP; this package uses several
deterministic starts and an exact active-set solve (see
\code{\link[=sc_weights_simplex]{sc_weights_simplex()}}). The \code{V} surface
is rough, so the two searches can land in \emph{different} local minima, and
either can find the better one. The objective is the same and is directly
comparable, so taking the better of the two is free accuracy and the
only defensible default when the surface is this rough.

When several convex combinations of donors fit equally well the
minimiser is not unique, and different solvers can return very different
comparison groups with the same loss. \code{penalty} resolves such ties;
without it, report \code{omega} rather than assuming it.

\strong{Bias correction.} Even a good synthetic control leaves a residual
discrepancy in the predictors, and that discrepancy biases the estimate.
The correction regresses the outcome on the predictors \emph{among the donor
units}, one regression per period, and subtracts the model's prediction
of the discrepancy. A ridge penalty keeps it defined when the positive-
weight donors are fewer than the predictors.

Note the diagnostic consequence, which is a feature and not a bug: \strong{in
any period whose outcome is itself a predictor, the corrected gap is
mechanically zero}, because the period's regression fits that column
exactly. Seeing a row of exact zeros in the gap path is confirmation that
the correction ran, not evidence of a perfect match. It is what produces
the flat segments at matched years in bias-corrected SCM gap figures.
}

